# Code owners — GITLAB dialect. The GitHub twin is `.github/CODEOWNERS`; the two files say
# the same thing in two syntaxes, and only one host ever reads each. A project supports
# exactly one host (see `docs/architecture.md`), so on any given repo one of these two files
# is dead weight — delete it rather than leaving it to rot into a lie about who reviews what.
#
# Template: replace `@OWNER` with your user or group. Read the note on `[…][2]` first.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE LIVES IN `.gitlab/` AND NOT AT THE REPO ROOT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitLab accepts CODEOWNERS in three places — the repo root, `docs/`, and `.gitlab/` — and
# uses the first one it finds. Keeping exactly one, here, is deliberate:
#
#   * The ROOT is shared ground. GitHub reads a root `CODEOWNERS` too, and GitHub's parser
#     has no concept of `^[Section]` or `[Section][2]`: it flags those lines as syntax
#     errors and the paths listed under them are guarded by nothing. A file that is silently
#     inert on the other host is worse than no file, because the settings page keeps showing
#     it and everyone assumes the paths are covered.
#   * `docs/` is actively harmful HERE, specifically. The `doc-sync` gate counts any change
#     under `docs/` as "documentation was updated". Put the ownership rules there and an MR
#     that changes nothing but who may approve what would satisfy the gate — a governance
#     change buying itself an exemption from the governance check.
#   * `.gitlab/` already holds `merge_request_templates/`. One host, one directory, mirroring
#     `.github/` — the path alone answers "which host is this for?".
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT ACTUALLY MAKES THIS BINDING (two switches, both outside this file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   1. Code Owners is a PREMIUM/ULTIMATE feature. On Free the file parses and renders, and
#      approvals are not enforced. A Free project gets a document, not a control.
#   2. Even on Premium, code-owner approval is only REQUIRED when the protected branch says
#      so: `code_owner_approval_required=true`, set per branch. See
#      `docs/configuration.md` § "Branch protection". Without it this file merely *suggests*
#      reviewers, which is the failure mode worth naming out loud — the rules look enforced,
#      the merge button stays green, and nobody discovers the difference until the review
#      that mattered was skipped.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECTIONS — the thing GitHub has no equivalent of, and why they are used below
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub CODEOWNERS is one flat list where the LAST matching pattern wins, and only that
# one owner set applies. That single rule is the trap: a catch-all `*` line placed after a
# specific path silently erases it, and the file still looks correct.
#
# GitLab evaluates each named section INDEPENDENTLY. A file can match a rule in three
# sections and then needs approval from all three. `*` in one section can no longer cancel
# a specific path in another, and each section carries its own required-approval count.
# That is exactly the shape of the intent here: everything needs an owner, and a few paths
# need more than that.
#
# Syntax notes for whoever edits this next:
#   `[Name][n] @owner`   required section; `n` distinct approvals; `@owner` is the default
#                        owner for every entry in it that names none.
#   `^[Name]`            OPTIONAL section — suggests reviewers, requires nothing. Not used
#                        below: every path here is load-bearing, and an optional rule on a
#                        load-bearing path is decoration.
#   Entries before the first section land in an implicit default section.
#
# ***THE `[2]` TRAP***: two required approvals means two DISTINCT eligible approvers. If
# `@OWNER` is substituted with a single user, every MR touching that section deadlocks —
# permanently, with no error message beyond "requires 2 approvals". Substitute a GROUP with
# at least two members there, or drop the `[2]` down to `[1]`. Do not leave a single-user
# owner behind a two-approval section and discover it during an incident fix.

# Everything has an owner. Without this, a path nobody listed is a path nobody reviews.
[Everything][1] @OWNER
*

# THE STANDARD ITSELF — the highest blast radius in this repository, and the reason for a
# second approver.
#
# `standard/` holds the rule sources and `CLAUDE.md` is the file they generate; every repo
# created from here VENDORS a copy of both. There is no version pin, no import graph and no
# compiler, so a bad edit here does not fail anyone's build — it silently desynchronises a
# dozen repositories and surfaces weeks later as a drift-gate failure in a repo whose author
# changed nothing. `templates/` is the same problem in a different shape: it is shipped
# verbatim into every spawned repo, so a mistake in a scaffold is a mistake in every
# repository created after it and in none created before.
#
# Two approvals is not ceremony. It is the only mechanism that exists between an edit here
# and twelve repositories inheriting it, because nothing downstream can reject it.
[The standard and what it spawns][2] @OWNER
/CLAUDE.md
/standard/
/templates/

# CI, GATES AND DEPENDENCY POLICY — the rules and CI are load-bearing.
#
# These files decide what "green" means. Someone who can weaken a gate can merge anything
# afterwards without ever touching the thing they wanted to merge, and the weakening is the
# part nobody re-reads. `renovate.json` belongs here for the same reason: it decides which
# third-party code enters this repo unattended and under which label — and the `dependencies`
# label is itself a doc-sync exemption.
#
# `.github/` stays listed even on a GitLab-hosted project: while both pipelines exist, an
# edit there still changes what this repository claims to enforce.
